# Install

Download the file for your system from the [download section](https://highercomve.github.io/GhostReel/#download)
or the [GitHub releases](https://github.com/highercomve/GhostReel/releases).

| Platform | File | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Windows 10/11 | `GhostReel_x.y.z_x64-setup.exe` | NVIDIA driver 570+ for the GPU (the CUDA 12.8 runtime is bundled) |
| Linux | `GhostReel_x.y.z_amd64.AppImage` | NVIDIA driver 570+ for the GPU |
| macOS 14+ · Apple Silicon | `GhostReel_x.y.z_aarch64.dmg` | MLX for vision and chat, Metal for Whisper |
| macOS 12+ · Intel | `GhostReel_x.y.z_x64.dmg` | llama.cpp and Whisper with Metal |

- **Windows**: run the installer.
- **Linux**: make the AppImage executable (`chmod +x GhostReel_*.AppImage`)
  and run it.
- **macOS**: open the DMG and drag **GhostReel** to **Applications**. The app
  is self-signed and not notarized by Apple, so the first launch is blocked:
  open **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Open Anyway**. Later releases
  reuse the same signing certificate.

ffmpeg and ffprobe are bundled, so there is nothing else to install.

## Updates

GhostReel checks for new releases on the **Status** page. Click **Check for
updates**: when a newer version is available you see the release notes and a
**Download and install** button. Once the download finishes, **Restart to
finish** applies the update. Updates are signed, and the app checks them with
a public key embedded in it.

# Set up the models

On first start, open **Models** and download what you need:

| Capability | Default model | Size | Alternatives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Speech | Whisper `large-v3-turbo` | 1.6 GB | `small` (488 MB), quantized turbo q8_0 / q5_0 (874 / 574 MB), `.en` variants |
| Frame descriptions & script chat | `Bonsai-27B` Q1_0 + projector | 3.8 GB | Qwen2.5-VL 7B (4.7 GB), Gemma 3 4B (2.5 GB), Qwen2.5-VL 3B (1.9 GB) |
| Search embeddings | `embeddinggemma-300M` Q8_0 | 334 MB | fixed, so indexes stay compatible between computers |

GhostReel also picks up models you already have, such as
[GhostPen](https://highercomve.github.io/GhostPen/)'s Whisper models and LM
Studio's models, instead of downloading them again. The default setup fits an
8 GB GPU.

On **Apple Silicon**, frame descriptions and the script chat run with MLX. The
Models page offers MLX versions of Qwen2.5-VL 3B and 7B, Gemma 3 4B and
Qwen3.5 9B; the initial vision model there is Qwen2.5-VL 3B. Interrupted
downloads resume, and a model is only marked installed once every file is
complete.

Frame descriptions and the script chat are configured separately: describing
a keyframe is a short prompt plus one image, while the chat needs room for
search results and a whole draft.

## Where each model runs

Each capability (speech, vision and chat, embeddings) is set to **Auto**,
**This computer** or **Server** on the **Models** page:

| | Standalone (default) | Shared servers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Speech | `ghostreel-asr` helper (whisper.cpp) | GhostPen's speech server |
| Vision & chat | `ghostreel-llm` helper (llama.cpp) | any OpenAI-compatible vision server, such as [highllama](https://github.com/highercomve/highllama) |
| Embeddings | `ghostreel-llm` helper | a server with the same embedding model |

*Auto* uses a server only when it is reachable **and capable** (vision
support, timestamped transcripts, the exact embedding model), and falls back
to the local helper otherwise. `ghostreel doctor` shows what would be used and
why. Running against servers you already have means the same GPU never holds
a model twice.

## A coding-agent CLI instead of a model

No GPU headroom for a vision model? Point a capability at a coding-agent CLI
you already have (**claude**, **agy** or **opencode**) on the Models page
(backend **CLI agent**), or from a terminal:

```
ghostreel config set vision.backend cli        # frame descriptions through a CLI
ghostreel config set vision.cli.tool claude
ghostreel config set chat_model.backend local  # …while the script chat stays local
ghostreel config test-cli vision               # one call, prints what it answered
```

The two capabilities are independent. **Auto never picks a CLI**: it spends
money or quota, so you have to choose it. Frame descriptions make one call per
keyframe, so check the cost for your library first; the Models page has a
**Test one call** button that checks the setup and the latency before you
index.

# Index your footage

1. **New project**: a name, a frame rate and a resolution (used for timeline
   exports).
2. **Add folder**: pick the footage folder. GhostReel adds the whole tree,
   finds every video and identifies it by its content, so moved or duplicated
   files are indexed once.
3. **Index now**. Progress shows in the project and on the **Activity** page.
   Stop any time; indexing again resumes where it left off.

Indexing transcribes the speech with timestamps and language detection,
extracts keyframes (scene changes, plus a frame at least every few seconds,
which you can configure) and describes each one (subject, setting, shot and
on-screen text), embeds everything for search, and measures camera movement
and handheld tremor so shaky stretches can be avoided.

```=html
<a id="search"></a>
```

# Search

Search the project in your own words: *people talking about the park*, *for
sale sign*, or text that appears on screen. Keyword search (SQLite FTS5) and
semantic search (embeddinggemma vectors in sqlite-vec) are merged into
**moments**. Click a hit to play from that moment; click a video to see its
keyframes and its transcript, and click a transcript line to jump there.

## Search selected footage

In a project's **Library**, check videos to search just those files.
Shift-click a checkbox for a range, Ctrl/⌘-click rows to add or remove files,
or turn on **Select videos** and drag a rectangle across rows. The header
checkbox selects all visible rows. **Clear selection** returns to the folder or
whole-project scope.

Choose a watched folder under **Search in**, or click **Search here** beside
its path, to restrict both the library and search to that folder's videos,
including its subfolders. Keyword and semantic search both honor these scopes.

```=html
<a id="scripts"></a>
```

# Script a video

Open the **Scripts** tab and describe the video you want, for example *a 60
second promo about the neighbourhood using the interviews*. The model explores
the footage with tools (search, transcripts, keyframes), drafts beats with
clips and voice-over, and revises from your feedback ("slower, let people
finish talking"). You can also edit beats and clips by hand; each change is a
new version.

- Every clip is checked against the footage, and talking clips keep whole
  sentences with a breath before the cut.
- The editor instructions the model follows are editable in **Settings**.
- Local script agents get a budget of research tool calls (30 by default),
  adjustable in Settings or right in the chat composer.

## Reference edits

Mark a finished video (for example, an edit someone already made by hand) as a
**reference**: the chat studies its length, pacing and structure, but never
uses it as footage. You can also remove a video from a project's library.

```=html
<a id="export"></a>
```

# Preview and export

**Render preview** renders a low-res preview of any script version, with
titles and narration burned in. Then export:

- **Export for Premiere (FCP XML)**: in Premiere Pro, *File → Import* the
  `.xml`; the sequence links to your original files.
- **Export .otio**: an OpenTimelineIO timeline.
- **Export MP4**: a video file to send as a quick demo.

```=html
<a id="mcp"></a>
```

# Command line

The `ghostreel` command line shares the app's core and covers indexing,
search, transcripts, scripts, exports and models. It is built from the
repository (see *Build from source* below):

```
ghostreel doctor                                   # GPU, ffmpeg, models, servers
ghostreel project create "Greet Mag" --fps 30 --width 3840 --height 2160
ghostreel folder add ~/Videos/shoot -p "Greet Mag"
ghostreel index -p "Greet Mag"                     # --watch to keep indexing new files
ghostreel search -p "Greet Mag" "neighbours talking about the community"
ghostreel script chat --project "Greet Mag" "a 60 second promo using the interviews"
ghostreel script export <script-id> --format fcp_xml -o promo.xml
ghostreel models list | download <id> | use <id>
ghostreel config set frames.max_interval_s 5 && ghostreel index -p "Greet Mag" --redo frames
```

Run `ghostreel --help` for everything else.

## For AI agents (MCP)

`ghostreel mcp` speaks the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io)
on stdio, so an agent can index footage, search it and build an edit. Register
it once:

```
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "ghostreel": { "command": "ghostreel", "args": ["mcp"] }
  }
}
```

In Claude Code: `claude mcp add ghostreel -- ghostreel mcp`. Add
`"env": {"GHOSTREEL_HOME": "/path"}` to work on a different library.

| Tool | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `doctor` | ffmpeg, GPU, database, models and which AI backends would be used |
| `list_projects` · `create_project` | projects with their indexing progress; create one |
| `add_folder` | watch a footage folder (the whole tree) |
| `index` · `index_status` | run pending indexing for a while (resumable), or report progress |
| `search` | hybrid keyword and meaning search; returns moments with timestamps |
| `get_transcript` · `get_video` | what people say, and what each keyframe shows |
| `list_scripts` · `get_script` | script versions of a project |
| `draft_script` | let GhostReel's editing model draft or revise a script |
| `save_script` | save your own script JSON as a new version; returns the issues found |
| `export_script` | write `fcp_xml` (Premiere Pro) or `otio` |
| `set_video_role` | take a video out of the library, or keep it as a reference edit |

A typical run: `create_project` → `add_folder` → `index` until nothing is
pending → `search`, `get_transcript` and `get_video` to learn the footage →
`draft_script` or `save_script` → `export_script`. Clips must lie inside
footage the tools reported: `save_script` returns an error per clip that
doesn't (and still saves the version, so it can be fixed).

# Where things are

Everything lives in one folder: `~/.ghostreel/` (`%USERPROFILE%\.ghostreel\`
on Windows). `GHOSTREEL_HOME` moves it.

| Path | What |
| --- | --- |
| `config.toml` | backends, models, frame interval, chat instructions |
| `ghostreel.db` | projects, transcripts, frame descriptions, vectors, scripts (SQLite) |
| `frames/` | keyframe JPEGs |
| `proxies/`, `previews/` | preview renders (safe to delete; regenerated) |
| `models/` | downloaded models |

## Privacy

Your videos are only read, never modified or copied.

- In **standalone** mode nothing leaves your machine.
- With **servers**, frames, transcripts and prompts go to the URLs you
  configured: point them at machines you trust. API keys are stored in plain
  text in `config.toml`.
- With a **coding-agent CLI**, that tool receives the frames or prompts for the
  capability you gave it.

# Build from source

Requirements: Rust (stable), Node.js 22, the
[Tauri prerequisites](https://v2.tauri.app/start/prerequisites/) and ffmpeg on
`PATH`. GPU helpers need the CUDA Toolkit 12.x (and MSVC on Windows). On macOS,
install the Xcode command-line tools and CMake; native helpers use Metal
automatically.

```
git clone https://github.com/highercomve/GhostReel
cd GhostReel
npm install
cargo test --workspace                 # core tests (no GPU, no servers)
cargo run -p ghostreel-cli -- doctor   # the ghostreel CLI
npx tauri dev                          # desktop app with hot reload
scripts/build-helpers.sh               # ghostreel-asr + ghostreel-llm (CUDA if the toolkit is present)
```

See the [README](https://github.com/highercomve/GhostReel#build-from-source)
for release bundles, Apple Silicon (MLX) builds and macOS signing.
